## 📋 Week 1 (Unit Tests)

### 4. Write Unit Tests
```bash
# Create test cases for:
cd vscode-extension

# Test files to update:
# - src/manager.test.ts (skeleton exists)
# - src/http-client.test.ts (skeleton exists)
# - src/server.test.ts (create new)

# Test coverage:
npm test -- --coverage

# Expected: 50%+ coverage across all files
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] `manager.ts`: Binary detection, spawn, lifecycle
- [ ] `http-client.ts`: HTTP calls, error handling
- [ ] `server.ts`: MCP tool mapping, request/response
- [ ] Coverage report generated
- [ ] All tests pass

### 5. Create E2E Test Harness
```bash
# Create e2e-test/ directory with:
# - test-fixture.poml (sample file)
# - test-runner.js (spawn VS Code, open file, verify MCP calls)

npm test integration.test.ts

# Expected: All integration tests pass
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Integration test file created
- [ ] Test fixtures included
- [ ] All MCP tools tested (6 tools)
- [ ] Server lifecycle tested
- [ ] Error scenarios covered

### 6. Cross-Platform Validation
```bash
# On macOS:
vscode-extension/bin/darwin/amd64/poml --help
vscode-extension/bin/darwin/arm64/poml --help

# On Linux (VM or CI):
vscode-extension/bin/linux/amd64/poml --help

# On Windows (VM or CI):
vscode-extension/bin/windows/amd64/poml.exe --help

# Expected: Each binary runs without errors
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] macOS Intel binary verified
- [ ] macOS ARM binary verified
- [ ] Linux Intel binary verified (if possible)
- [ ] Windows binary verified (if possible)
- [ ] No dependency issues (statically linked)

