## 🎨 Week 2 (Polish & UX)

### 7. Create Extension Icon
**Specifications:**
- Dimensions: 128×128 pixels (minimum)
- Format: PNG or SVG
- Colors: Match VS Code dark/light theme
- Placement: `resources/icon.png`

**Reference:**
- VS Code icon guidelines: https://code.visualstudio.com/api/extension-guides/overview
- Example: Use Atlas Foundry logo or POML-themed icon

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Icon created (128×128)
- [ ] Icon placed in `resources/icon.png`
- [ ] Icon displays correctly in marketplace
- [ ] Icon visible in VS Code extension sidebar

### 8. Capture Marketplace Screenshots
**Requirements:**
- 1–5 screenshots showing:
  1. Extension installed in VS Code
  2. `.poml` file open
  3. Server running (status bar indicator)
  4. Output panel with server logs
  5. MCP client using the server (optional)

**Specifications:**
- Dimensions: 1200×750 minimum
- Format: PNG
- Placement: `screenshots/` directory

**Checklist:**
- [ ] 3–5 screenshots captured
- [ ] Screenshots show key features
- [ ] Screenshots have captions
- [ ] Screenshots look professional

### 9. Add Configuration UI
```typescript
// In package.json, add:
"configuration": {
  "title": "POML MCP Server",
  "properties": {
    "poml.binaryPath": {
      "type": "string",
      "default": "",
      "description": "Path to poml binary (leave empty to auto-detect)"
    },
    "poml.port": {
      "type": "number",
      "default": 7777,
      "description": "Port for POML HTTP server"
    },
    "poml.enableTracing": {
      "type": "boolean",
      "default": false,
      "description": "Enable OpenTelemetry stdout tracing"
    }
  }
}
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Configuration properties added to `package.json`
- [ ] `manager.ts` reads settings
- [ ] Settings UI appears in VS Code Settings
- [ ] Settings are applied on restart

### 10. Add Output Channel
```typescript
// In src/extension.ts:
const outputChannel = vscode.window.createOutputChannel("POML MCP");
// Forward all logs to output channel
binaryManager.on("stdout", (data) => outputChannel.append(data));
binaryManager.on("stderr", (data) => outputChannel.append(data));
outputChannel.show();
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Output channel created
- [ ] Server logs forwarded
- [ ] Output visible in VS Code Output panel
- [ ] Channel auto-opens on errors

---

## 🔗 Week 3 (Registry Integration)

### 11. VS Code Marketplace Setup
```bash
# 1. Create Microsoft account (if needed)
# 2. Visit https://dev.azure.com/_usersSettings/tokens
# 3. Create Personal Access Token (PAT)
#    - Scopes: Marketplace > Manage
#    - Expiration: 90 days
# 4. Copy token to clipboard

# 5. Create publisher (one-time):
npx vsce create-publisher atlas-foundry

# 6. Set GitHub secret:
# GitHub Repo → Settings → Secrets & Variables → Actions
# New secret: VSCODE_MARKETPLACE_TOKEN = <PAT>
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Microsoft account created/verified
- [ ] PAT token created
- [ ] Publisher account created
- [ ] GitHub secret configured

### 12. Open VSX Setup
```bash
# 1. Visit https://open-vsx.org
# 2. Sign up with GitHub/email
# 3. Navigate to user settings
# 4. Create Personal Access Token
# 5. Copy token

# 6. Set GitHub secret:
# GitHub Repo → Settings → Secrets & Variables → Actions
# New secret: OVSX_TOKEN = <token>
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Open VSX account created
- [ ] PAT token created
- [ ] GitHub secret configured

### 13. MCP Registry Registration
```bash
# After VS Code Marketplace publication:
# 1. Visit https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/submit
# 2. Fill out submission form:
#    - Name: POML MCP Server
#    - Type: Server
#    - Repository: https://github.com/atlas-foundry/poml-go-sdk
#    - Installation: npx @atlas-foundry/poml-mcp
# 3. Submit for review

# Timeline: 1–2 weeks for approval
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Marketplace ready for submission
- [ ] Submission form filled out accurately
- [ ] Repository public and documented
- [ ] Installation command tested

---

## 🚀 Week 4 (Launch)

### 14. Final Testing Before Release
```bash
# Checklist:
# - [ ] All unit tests passing (npm test)
# - [ ] All E2E tests passing
# - [ ] ESLint passes (npm run lint)
# - [ ] TypeScript strict mode passes
# - [ ] No console errors in debug mode
# - [ ] Extension packages successfully (npm run package)
# - [ ] .vsix can be installed manually
# - [ ] All 6 platform binaries work
# - [ ] Documentation complete and accurate
# - [ ] CHANGELOG updated
```

### 15. Create Release
```bash
# 1. Update version in package.json:
# "version": "0.1.0"

# 2. Update CHANGELOG.md:
# ## [0.1.0] - 2025-11-30
# ### Added
# - Initial release with cross-platform binary bundling
# - MCP server with 6 tools (validate, convert, search, patch, diagram, ast)
# - Auto-activation and status bar integration

# 3. Commit and tag:
cd poml-go-sdk
git add .
git commit -m "chore: release v0.1.0"
git tag v0.1.0
git push origin main --tags

# GitHub Actions will automatically:
# - Build all binaries
# - Package extension
# - Create Release
# - Publish to marketplaces
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] Version bumped in `package.json`
- [ ] CHANGELOG updated
- [ ] Git tag created
- [ ] Push triggers CI/CD

### 16. Verify Marketplace Publication
```bash
# 1. Check GitHub Release page:
# https://github.com/atlas-foundry/poml-go-sdk/releases/tag/v0.1.0
# - Should show .vsix artifact

# 2. Check VS Code Marketplace (24 hours):
# https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=atlas-foundry.poml-mcp
# - Should show version 0.1.0
# - Should show icon, screenshots, description

# 3. Check Open VSX (typically 1 hour):
# https://open-vsx.org/extension/atlas-foundry/poml-mcp
# - Should show version 0.1.0

# 4. Test installation from marketplace:
# Open VS Code → Extensions → Search "poml" → Install
```

**Checklist:**
- [ ] GitHub Release created
- [ ] VS Code Marketplace updated
- [ ] Open VSX Registry updated
- [ ] Test install successful
- [ ] Server starts without errors

